Ethics notes

    • mundane design by cosmic creators
        : see @ ../text.brec
        !! what is qualified cannot ground the absolute principle that is moral law
            : re `(absolute principle) that is (moral law)` see `^*: re .${2}.+${1}`s @ ../text.brec
            - the creators, in being contingent, might be taken as qualified in a sense
              that makes them unfit to ground moral law
            | refute the premise: the mundane cosmic creators are not so qualified
                ∵ neither is the practical ground of their creative act, whether default or not,
                  so qualified
                    ∵ the default practical ground merely *contains* a qualification
                      as opposed to being *governed* by one
                        - it applies (its qualified content) unconditionally and universally
            | elevate the qualification to the law
                - let the qualified instead ground a qualified|contingent|default moral law
              \ | let the scope of the moral law be cosmic, not universal
              \     / thus a cosmic moral law, the moral law of our cosmos, so qualified
              \     ∵ within the context of their creation, even mundane creators are necessary
              \\    !! then cosmic creation would be amoral, out of moral scope
    - two things suffice to ground an ethic: the phenomenon of the cosmic safehold
      and the concept of moral law

               cosmic safehold  + ‘moral law’ →  ethic

        : see @ ../text.brec
        + repair ∵ they cannot themselves ground (let alone suffice to)
          if only ∵ at least one is contingent
            | rather they conduct [us|one] {to the [theoretic] grounds}|{in the [theoretic] grounding}
              by locating|pinpointing the [theoretic] grounds (and suffice to)

                   (cosmic safehold, ‘moral law’) → location and existence of
                   (cosmic creator, truth of reason)

                   cosmic creator + truth of reason →
                   satisfaction of conditions of moral law

                   but:
                   satisfaction of conditions of moral law =
                   [theoretic] grounding of an ethic

                   whereby:
                   cosmic creator + truth of reason →
                   [theoretic] grounds of an ethic

            | rather they pinpoint|locate the [theoretic] grounds (and suffice to)
    + begin (this philosophic frame) with (or involving) a search for the creator’s reason
        : re `creator’s reason` see `^*• God had reason to make the safehold$`s
          @ ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
        : re `creator’s reason` see `^*• cosmocrafters have reason to.+cosmocraft lineally`s
          @ ~/work/safehold/aetiology/text.brec
        but no+
            ∵ the normative ethic is the proper place for seeking the creator’s reason
                : re `^*∵ (.+)$` see `^*∴ ${same}$` @
                  `^*- the command condition is proper to normative ethics$`
        wanted first+ is a (prior) reason to do that
            - the opportunity (presented by the safehold) to meet ‘Anscombe’s challenge’
              of a modern (re)grounding for moral law
                : re `‘Anscombe’s challenge’` see doi:10.1017/9781139025065 : Darwall, Stephen.  2023.
                  *Modern moral philosophy from Grotius to Kant.*  pp. 1-4
                : re `‘Anscombe’s challenge’` see doi:10.1017/9781009543835 : Darwall, Stephen.  2025.
                  *Modern moral philosophy in the nineteenth century.*  pp. 1-3
                - the logic of so doing, then, informs the text
                    : cf. ~/work/safehold/aetiology/._/01.logic_tour/
                - to read: \ in addition to Anscombe and Darwall
                    \ 1958.  Anscombe, G.E.M.  ‘Modern moral philosophy.’
                    + 1958.  Remnant, Peter.  ‘God and the moral law.’
                        : see https://biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/cjt/04-1_023.pdf
                    + 2011.  Murphy, Mark.  *God and moral law: on the theistic explanation of morality.*
                        : see doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199693665.001.0001
                    + 2017.  Rosen, Gideon.  *What is a moral law?.*
                        : see doi:10.1093/oso/9780198805076.003.0006
                    + 2022.  Driver, Julia.  *Moral theory.*
                        : see https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-theory/
                    \ 2023.  Darwall, Stephen.  *Modern moral philosophy from Grotius to Kant.*
                    \ 2025.  Darwall, Stephen.  *Modern moral philosophy in the nineteenth century.*
                    + re "Anscombe" "Modern moral philosophy"
                wanted first+ again, is a (prior) reason to do that
                    - analysis of the concept of moral law (on the evidence of the safehold)
                      suffices to ground the norms and facts (application) of an ethic
                        - given that, we have no need of (the field of) metaethics
                            : pace ../meta/
                            + so say there
                    - the two conditions of moral law (that analysis yields) apportion cleanly between
                      the two remaining fields of ethics (normative and applied)
                        - the command condition is proper to normative ethics
                            ∴ the normative ethic is the proper place for seeking the creator’s reason
                        - the enforcement condition is proper to applied ethics
                            ∵ outside the norms that guide, the only question is the facts
                              that move us to conform
                        - division of concept into conditions → division of ethics into fields
                            + make consideration of the latter *follow* on that of the former
                                / rather than consider first the customary division of ethics,
                                  then, in that light, the division of the concept
                                - the customary fields of ethics I reuse if (and as) applicable
                                    : ad `\(and as\)` : I wear the shoe as it fits
                    frame curtly+ aiming for extent (as proper to a frame) over content
    + argue for my preferred safehold explanantia here in the ethic (alone),
      where they figure as a premise
        : re `explanantia` see `be agnostic re design explanantia, not preferring one.+over others` @
          ../applied/._/text.notes.brec
        : re `here in the ethic \(alone\)` cf. `so much for the aetiology: no need to argue here` @
          ~/work/safehold/aetiology/._/text.notes_boneyard.brec
        - argue not on theoretic|epistemic grounds
            / chapter 1 of Waller 2020 convinces me there are none at present
            - nor on metaphysical grounds
                / contra Waller 2020, ∵ metaphysics mystifies me
        - rather argue on the practical|methodological ground of my preferred explanantia
          being the best|only on which to premise a normative ethic
            ?+ better warrant (more encouraging) to set aside the others, is there?
                | forget it
                    | the others and their ilk must, at present, hang over and threaten
                      all moral endeavours
                        | there is no theoretic|epistemic ground
                            / chapter 1 of Waller 2020 convinces me there are none at present
                                : N.B.
                        | uncertainty inheres in the one ground that we (at present) can locate
                | no choice at present re the others
                    | they have no moral bearing (at present) precisely ∵
                      they rest on physics ‘yet unknown’
                    | all (at present) rest on physics ‘yet unknown’
                | weighing the possible courses of moral action
                    | productive|substantial vs. mere waiting (for physics)
                | absent the (present) possibility of theoretic warrant, fall back to practical
                    ?+ where did Kant argue for practical warrant in such cases?
                    ?+ does anyone else argue for it?
                    ∵ one has neither practical warrant for the others (all being amoral),
                      nor (at present) theoretic (all resting on physics ‘yet unknown’)
    moral law, conditions
        : note: these according to one or another of the various divine-command theories of morality,
          divorced of its supernatural assumptions, with the creator in place of God
        a) an absolute principle defining the criteria of right action
            : re `^*a. (an absolute principle defining the criteria of right action)$`
              see `^*: re .moral law.+${same}`s @ ~/work/ethics/text.brec
            : join @ `^^moral law, conditions that hold$`i @ ../default/._/text.notes.brec
        b) its command (addressed demand) by our creator
            : cf. `^*: re .moral law.+a divine ordinance`s @ ~/work/ethics/text.brec
            : join @ `^^moral law, conditions that hold$`i @ ../normative/._/text.notes.brec
        c) the creator’s backing of it by force
            : join @ `^^moral law, conditions that hold$`i @ ../applied/._/text.notes.brec
